WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:15.660
- Hello, welcome to our
daily homeroom live stream.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.140
For those of you all this
is your first time coming,

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:19.415
this is something that we started doing

00:00:19.415 --> 00:00:22.100
when we started seeing school
closures around the world.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:23.800
Khan Academy, we are not for profit

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.280
with a mission of providing
a free world class education

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:27.797
for anyone anywhere.

00:00:27.797 --> 00:00:30.540
Obviously a big part of
that is we've been creating

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:33.110
learning materials, software, videos,

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:35.340
all free, all not commercial.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.150
And as soon as we saw the
school closures happening,

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:39.760
we said we have to make
this as usable as possible

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:41.710
for teachers, parents, and students

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:43.682
so that everyone can keep learning.

00:00:43.682 --> 00:00:46.150
So that's when we started
publishing schedules,

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:48.493
putting out learning plans,
parent and teacher webinars,

00:00:48.493 --> 00:00:51.645
and we also thought, in a
time of social distancing,

00:00:51.645 --> 00:00:53.410
wouldn't it be nice if there was a place

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:55.470
where we could connect in
a, I guess you could say,

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:58.360
synchronous way, where we
could have conversations,

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:01.020
answer each other's questions,
bring on interesting guests.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.670
And that's what this live stream is for.

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:06.270
And now before we get in to
the meat of the live stream,

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:08.900
and the meat of today's live stream

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:10.490
will be around Giving Tuesday.

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:11.820
There's actually a lot of things going on.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:14.910
It's National Teacher Appreciation Week.

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:16.040
We'll talk a little bit about that.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:17.290
But it's also Giving Tuesday.

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:18.546
But in line with Giving Tuesday,

00:01:18.546 --> 00:01:20.980
I do wanna remind everyone, again,

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:22.130
we are not for profit.

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:24.900
And the only way we're
able to do our work,

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.980
for it to be free, for
it to be non commercial.

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:28.310
A lot of people come to Khan Academy

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:29.573
and they think there's a catch.

00:01:29.573 --> 00:01:32.210
Clearly there's a lot of
resources being put into it.

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:34.680
Are they going to try to sell
me something at some point?

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:36.826
The reason why we don't,
and we never intend to

00:01:36.826 --> 00:01:39.618
is because we're supported
with philanthropic donations

00:01:39.618 --> 00:01:41.100
from folks like yourself,

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:42.780
and we'll talk a lot
about that over the course

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.300
of today's conversation.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.140
But I do wanna give special thanks

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.102
to several corporations
that have stepped up

00:01:49.102 --> 00:01:51.250
in recent weeks when they found out

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:53.420
that we were already running at a deficit

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:55.627
before the COVID crisis,
and now in the crisis

00:01:55.627 --> 00:01:58.000
with our traffic being two to three X

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.170
of what it typically is, and
we're trying to accelerate

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:02.040
all of these programs to
figure out better ways

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:03.940
to support parents,
teachers, and students.

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:08.050
Bank of America, Google.org, AT&amp;T,

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:09.820
Novartis, and fastly all stepped up,

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:11.640
literally in a matter of days,

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.770
to make sure that we have less of a gap.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:15.240
But we still need more help.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.490
I wanna make it very clear.

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:19.452
Khan Academy is much more than just me.

00:02:19.452 --> 00:02:23.830
There's 200 full-time
teachers, researchers,

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:26.463
engineers, designers, managers,

00:02:26.463 --> 00:02:29.100
who are bringing Khan Academy to you

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:30.557
and because of that we're able to serve,

00:02:30.557 --> 00:02:33.610
we have over 100 million registered users.

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:35.541
There's 10s of millions
of learners who come

00:02:35.541 --> 00:02:37.430
on a monthly basis.

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:41.000
And that's only possible with
financial support donations.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.150
So given that today is Giving Tuesday,

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:45.580
that's going to be the focus of this,

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:47.711
but this will be a two-way exchange.

00:02:47.711 --> 00:02:50.100
I am going to try to convince you,

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:50.970
if you're in a position.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:52.336
I don't wanna guilt you if
you're not in a position

00:02:52.336 --> 00:02:54.860
to do so, but every dollar matters.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:56.130
But if you're in a position to do so,

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:57.353
please think about donating.

00:02:57.353 --> 00:03:00.515
And in return, the main thing in return,

00:03:00.515 --> 00:03:02.880
all of us at Khan Academy
are going to be able

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:05.049
to do our work and hopefully
serve you and many millions

00:03:05.049 --> 00:03:07.250
of students around the world.

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:09.391
But I'm also happy to answer any questions

00:03:09.391 --> 00:03:11.850
that you might have about anything.

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:14.320
So this is a true ask me anything.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:16.360
And so whether you're watching on Facebook

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:19.940
or on YouTube, put your
questions in the message boards.

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:22.370
Myself and some team members,
we're gonna be looking at that

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:25.610
and as long as it's decent
and nothing is out of bounds.

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:29.620
I'm happy to answer any question.

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:33.040
So while we're waiting
for questions to come in,

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:37.420
I'm just going to, we have
some goals with our team.

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:39.943
We're hoping to get 20,000 donations.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:42.700
We care about the number
of people donating

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:44.530
because that shows that
there's other people

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:45.420
in this boat with us.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:47.221
There's other people
around the world who care

00:03:47.221 --> 00:03:49.315
about this mission of
free world class education

00:03:49.315 --> 00:03:50.940
for anyone anywhere.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:52.930
Even if they're donating three dollars.

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:55.540
That makes a difference, and
it's a huge show of support

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:57.280
for the work that we're doing.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:02.280
Our goal is to raise $50,000
on YouTube in the month of May,

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:05.380
and $25,000 on Facebook this week.

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:07.450
If you look, I believe it's on my left,

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:09.890
I think I'm pointing in the right place,

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:11.970
you will see donate
buttons, either on Facebook

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:14.330
or YouTube where you'll have
an opportunity to donate.

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:15.913
Once again, if you're
in a position to do so,

00:04:15.913 --> 00:04:18.580
please think about doing that.

00:04:18.580 --> 00:04:22.970
And I will read some quick testimonials

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:25.560
just to give a sense of,
a flavor of how people

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:28.050
are using Khan Academy right now.

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:29.760
This is the type of thing
that gives us energy

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.250
and I hope all of you
all, if you view yourself

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:34.870
as part of the Khan Academy
community, which I hope you do,

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:36.070
especially if you're a supporter,

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:39.450
that you take satisfaction
in things like this.

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:41.883
This is a thing we got recently.

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:46.660
This here is a quote from
a physician, actually.

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:48.680
I'm a resident physician in New York.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:51.480
We've been working long, hard
days with COVID patients.

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:53.363
I'm writing to say thank you.

00:04:53.363 --> 00:04:57.750
I used your early materials to get through

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:00.310
my undergraduate and medical education.

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:02.380
My two and a half year old has been home

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:04.760
due to school closures
and I haven't had a chance

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:07.400
to see her much in the last few weeks.

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:08.809
I came home today and she said,

00:05:08.809 --> 00:05:11.940
let's sing the Khan Academy song

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:15.130
in regards to a song that has
helped her learn to count.

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:18.030
Your educational programs
carried me through my education

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:20.510
and are now teaching my
young daughter, thank you.

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:22.390
That's John, and so first of all,

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:24.090
John, if you're listening
or maybe you'll watch

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:26.324
the recording of this later,
thank you so much for that.

00:05:26.324 --> 00:05:29.140
One, the work you're doing.

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:32.520
Medical workers are genuinely
on the front line of this war,

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:34.660
whatever you wanna call it, against COVID,

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:36.350
so thank you for doing that work.

00:05:36.350 --> 00:05:38.560
And it makes us incredibly happy

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:40.870
and incredibly proud that
not only were we able

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:42.290
to be part of your education.

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:44.930
So in our way by us
helping you many years ago,

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:46.990
maybe you're going to be able to help

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:49.300
that many more people with COVID.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:51.190
But also in this time of need,

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:52.880
and I'm sure your family needs support

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:54.780
and your family's going
through a lot of stress,

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:56.799
it's our honor to be able
to support your daughter

00:05:56.799 --> 00:05:58.740
through her learning.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:00.030
And for those of you who don't know,

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:03.010
Khan Academy Kids, I had Caroline over,

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:04.840
who's the founder of Khan Academy Kids

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:06.750
last Friday on the live stream.

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:09.690
I can't speak highly
enough how cool it is.

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:11.160
I have a five and a half year old.

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:12.639
They just added first grade content.

00:06:12.639 --> 00:06:15.140
They also just added teacher tools,

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:16.660
so teachers can make assignments and see

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:18.110
what students have completed.

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:19.950
And it starts as early as ages two.

00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:21.660
It goes up to ages seven.

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:25.488
And I really think that
is, it's a math, reading,

00:06:25.488 --> 00:06:27.360
writing, social emotional urging.

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:29.310
It even has stuff on logic.

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:31.510
It covers all of the
standards that can be covered

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:32.420
in that format.

00:06:32.420 --> 00:06:34.610
I encourage parents or
anyone in your family

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:35.840
who might have an early learner

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:37.260
to check that out.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:39.280
And that's the type of
work that we're able to do

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:40.990
with this support.

00:06:40.990 --> 00:06:42.894
This all to cost resources,

00:06:42.894 --> 00:06:44.900
and whether it's the core Khan Academy

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:46.410
that many of you all are familiar with

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:50.185
or Khan Academy Kids, it's your donations

00:06:50.185 --> 00:06:53.190
that allow us to build that, support that,

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:55.840
support 10s of millions
of users every month.

00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:59.420
And to continue to improve on that.

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:00.333
So let's see.

00:07:00.333 --> 00:07:02.893
We have questions here.

00:07:04.096 --> 00:07:09.096
One from Instagram, Sarah
Hellisleesleepy, I think.

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:15.860
What motivated you to build Khan Academy?

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:17.520
So I've told this story a little bit,

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:20.230
but I'll tell it again in a fast form.

00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:22.840
I was tutoring cousins back in 2004.

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:25.470
My day job, I was an analyst
at an investment firm.

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:28.100
And I just saw that maybe
there was some tools

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:30.500
that I could help build
that will help family.

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:32.733
So that's where I started
writing the exercise platform.

00:07:32.733 --> 00:07:34.350
A very early version of it.

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:36.660
For my cousins to get practice, feedback.

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:38.650
For me as their teacher or their tutor

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:40.680
to keep track of what they were doing.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:42.590
And then a few years
later a friend suggested,

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:44.510
hey why don't you make video lessons

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:47.490
to supplement this software
thing you're making.

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:48.840
And I initially thought
it was a horrible idea.

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:50.989
And I was, cats playing piano.

00:07:50.989 --> 00:07:53.430
YouTube, it's not for serious math.

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:54.500
But I got over the idea.

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:56.500
It wasn't my idea, and I gave it a shot.

00:07:56.500 --> 00:07:58.890
And long story short, I
made it for my family,

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:00.660
but it soon became clear that people

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:03.330
who are not my family were watching.

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:06.860
And by 2008, I said this
could be a real thing,

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:09.030
and that's where I
incorporated Khan Academy

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:10.060
as a not for profit.

00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:10.990
And for those of you who don't know

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:14.300
what a not for profit is, it
means no one owns Khan Academy.

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:15.640
It is a public charity.

00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:16.960
I do not own Khan Academy.

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:18.440
Or another way to think about it, you own

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:20.580
as much of Khan Academy as I do.

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:22.310
I do get a salary from Khan Academy.

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:24.840
All of our team members get
salaries from Khan Academy.

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:26.850
But we are employees of this organization.

00:08:26.850 --> 00:08:28.620
It's governed by a board,

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:31.630
and that board, it's in the
for profit organization,

00:08:31.630 --> 00:08:34.560
the board is, how do we
maximize shareholder value?

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:36.660
In a not for profit board, the board says,

00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:38.590
how do we maximize impact.

00:08:38.590 --> 00:08:40.480
Especially impact
aligned with our mission,

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:42.434
which is a free world class education

00:08:42.434 --> 00:08:44.530
for anyone anywhere.

00:08:44.530 --> 00:08:47.770
And by 2009, I was
hoping at first to set up

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:50.050
as a not for profit, maybe
some friends would volunteer

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:51.560
to help me out.

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:54.663
But by 2009, I had trouble
focusing on my day job.

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:58.370
My first child was just born.

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:00.180
My wife was a medical
resident, so we're making

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:02.660
a little bit of money, but
I decided to quit my job

00:09:02.660 --> 00:09:04.050
and we lived off of savings.

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:07.130
We were digging in to our
savings, about $5,000 a month.

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:09.780
Just under the belief
that this needed to exist

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:10.680
in the world.

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:13.410
Back then there was about
100,000 people using Khan Academy

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:14.243
on a monthly basis.

00:09:14.243 --> 00:09:15.610
And I used to say, who knows?

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:18.950
Maybe one day it'll be a
million, 10 million, 100 million.

00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:22.480
And at the time those seemed
like delusional ideas,

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:24.200
but those are now true.

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:25.710
And I think of the opportunity and I said,

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:27.890
maybe one day billions
could learn from this.

00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:31.050
And imagine if future generations,

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:33.650
they don't view education
as something that only

00:09:33.650 --> 00:09:36.030
the affluent have or
only certain people have.

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:37.540
But they say as long as I have at least

00:09:37.540 --> 00:09:40.740
a low-cost smartphone, I can have access

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:42.530
to the world's best material.

00:09:42.530 --> 00:09:44.320
I have practice, I have feedback,

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:47.410
and I can learn everything
from early learning and math,

00:09:47.410 --> 00:09:49.150
reading, writing, all
the way through the core

00:09:49.150 --> 00:09:52.420
of elementary, middle,
high school, college.

00:09:52.420 --> 00:09:54.670
Not just in math, not just in ELA,

00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:56.250
but the social sciences, the sciences,

00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:58.210
and eventually plug in to the world.

00:09:58.210 --> 00:10:00.070
Imagine if we could
create a world like that.

00:10:00.070 --> 00:10:02.120
And so that was the hope of Khan Academy.

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:04.270
That's why it was a not for profit.

00:10:04.270 --> 00:10:07.245
And that's why, I hope,
that you're also excited

00:10:07.245 --> 00:10:10.000
about that type of a
vision of what we can do

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:11.480
for the world together.

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:13.880
I hope one day all of you are
able to tell your children

00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:15.702
or your grandchildren,
yeah there was a time

00:10:15.702 --> 00:10:18.866
where free world class
education wasn't a thing.

00:10:18.866 --> 00:10:21.210
What zip code you were born in

00:10:21.210 --> 00:10:23.680
or how much your family
made, that was the biggest

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:25.570
predictor of what you're
going to do in your life,

00:10:25.570 --> 00:10:27.960
which unfortunately, it
is the case right now.

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:29.710
But we think we can change that,

00:10:29.710 --> 00:10:31.130
and empower everyone.

00:10:31.130 --> 00:10:32.850
And that will not only
level the playing field,

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:37.850
but think about how many Marie
Curies or Albert Einsteins,

00:10:38.290 --> 00:10:39.840
for every one that we discover,

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:41.240
how many go undiscovered?

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:43.099
Think about all of the
work that those kids,

00:10:43.099 --> 00:10:45.800
who might be living in a
slum some place in India

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:49.010
right now, if they're able
to tap into their potential,

00:10:49.010 --> 00:10:50.500
what they might be able to do for us.

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:54.070
They might find the vaccine
for the next pandemic,

00:10:54.070 --> 00:10:57.090
a cure for a disease,
write the next great novel,

00:10:57.090 --> 00:11:00.470
solve existential problems for humanity.

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:03.930
So that's kind of the
dream of Khan Academy

00:11:03.930 --> 00:11:06.670
and how I got started.

00:11:06.670 --> 00:11:11.670
Let's see, we have more questions here.

00:11:11.890 --> 00:11:15.300
From Instagram, @sarah.girl2020 saying,

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:17.200
I'm having trouble staying focused

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:18.353
on my online classes.

00:11:18.353 --> 00:11:21.370
How can I be a little bit better?

00:11:21.370 --> 00:11:22.920
Good question, Sarah.

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:24.000
I'll tell you.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:26.020
Well there's a couple
things that I've been doing.

00:11:26.020 --> 00:11:28.030
One is love to keep moving.

00:11:28.030 --> 00:11:29.845
So you can imagine I
sit in a lot of meetings

00:11:29.845 --> 00:11:32.800
these days, and now they're
ll over Zoom and Google Meet

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:34.460
and whatever else.

00:11:34.460 --> 00:11:35.870
What I've been doing,
is actually I've been

00:11:35.870 --> 00:11:37.240
doing them while walking.

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:39.420
And you'll be surprised, I'm surprised.

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:41.620
You can be on a phone in a Zoom meeting

00:11:41.620 --> 00:11:44.360
and you can still see the
screen and see what's going on.

00:11:44.360 --> 00:11:46.810
I find if I'm just in a park,
ideally you're in some place

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:47.830
where there's not any traffic,

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:49.350
so you can't get hit by a car.

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:52.320
But I go to my local park
and I just keep doing laps

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:53.500
while I'm in the meeting.

00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:55.780
And I find that that
helps me stay focused.

00:11:55.780 --> 00:11:56.940
It gives me exercise.

00:11:56.940 --> 00:11:59.190
I get fresh air, which
is especially important

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:00.450
in this time of social distancing.

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:02.670
So if you're lucky enough
to have a safe environment

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:05.459
to walk in, I think that's another thing.

00:12:05.459 --> 00:12:10.459
I think a lot of educators
are trying to figure

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:12.553
things out right now.

00:12:12.553 --> 00:12:14.390
So I think if you give
constructive feedback,

00:12:14.390 --> 00:12:16.660
saying, hey, teacher,
that was a great session.

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:18.060
I really appreciate it,

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:20.490
but I would love to see
even more interactivity.

00:12:20.490 --> 00:12:23.460
I would love ways, there's
ways that we can participate.

00:12:23.460 --> 00:12:24.930
You can ask us questions.

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:27.251
I think a lot of educators
would welcome that.

00:12:27.251 --> 00:12:29.790
They want you to participate more.

00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:32.020
So that's another way
to stay really engaged.

00:12:32.020 --> 00:12:34.130
In general, whether
it's online or off line

00:12:34.130 --> 00:12:35.470
or just work you have to
do and there's something

00:12:35.470 --> 00:12:36.680
called a Pomodoro Technique,

00:12:36.680 --> 00:12:37.650
which we've talked about before.

00:12:37.650 --> 00:12:40.710
It's named after, a Pomodoro
is a type of tomato,

00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:42.847
but it's named after a
timer that was shaped

00:12:42.847 --> 00:12:45.270
like a Pomodoro tomato.

00:12:45.270 --> 00:12:48.220
The general idea is you
give yourself 20 minutes,

00:12:48.220 --> 00:12:49.810
30 minutes, you put it
on the timer and you say,

00:12:49.810 --> 00:12:52.730
okay, over that time, I'm
gonna work on whatever it is.

00:12:52.730 --> 00:12:54.480
And then when that timer rings,

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:56.700
you give yourself a 10 minute break.

00:12:56.700 --> 00:12:59.410
10 minutes to take a walk,
do some jumping jacks,

00:12:59.410 --> 00:13:00.340
dance a little bit.

00:13:00.340 --> 00:13:02.737
Whatever, and then get back to work.

00:13:02.737 --> 00:13:04.353
And by doing it in those sessions,

00:13:04.353 --> 00:13:07.410
you might think that 10 minutes
is kind of a waste of time

00:13:07.410 --> 00:13:08.890
where you're taking a break, but it's not.

00:13:08.890 --> 00:13:11.790
You're rejuvenating and
it'll give you more energy

00:13:11.790 --> 00:13:13.030
to then focus and go deep

00:13:13.030 --> 00:13:15.820
on whatever you're trying to work on.

00:13:15.820 --> 00:13:18.090
So more testimonials.

00:13:18.090 --> 00:13:20.710
And I actually wanna give
a shout out to thank yous

00:13:20.710 --> 00:13:22.380
on several of the donations.

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:26.693
Elliot H., on YouTube, thank
you so much for your donation.

00:13:27.547 --> 00:13:31.940
Sjwetel, if I'm reading
your username correctly.

00:13:31.940 --> 00:13:32.773
Thank you so much.

00:13:32.773 --> 00:13:34.390
Just donated $100 on YouTube.

00:13:34.390 --> 00:13:37.130
Thank so much Sjwetel, and Sjwetel writes,

00:13:37.130 --> 00:13:38.493
thank you Sal and
everyone at Khan Academy.

00:13:38.493 --> 00:13:39.970
It's a noble cause.

00:13:39.970 --> 00:13:42.510
The more knowledge we all have,
the more humane we become.

00:13:42.510 --> 00:13:47.130
And I apologize if I'm not
pronouncing your name correctly,

00:13:47.130 --> 00:13:50.540
or your username: sjwetel or shwetel.

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:53.870
Couldn't agree with you
more and I will emphasize,

00:13:53.870 --> 00:13:56.660
I get a disproportionate amount
of credit for Khan Academy.

00:13:56.660 --> 00:13:58.930
There's 200 plus full-time folks.

00:13:58.930 --> 00:14:01.130
About five or six of them are
actually behind the scenes

00:14:01.130 --> 00:14:05.020
right now, supporting this live stream.

00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:08.020
And beyond that, we have
thousands of volunteers

00:14:08.020 --> 00:14:09.000
around the world.

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:12.880
And that show of confidence
through that donation

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:13.713
makes a huge difference.

00:14:13.713 --> 00:14:17.070
And that donation is going
to make a huge difference.

00:14:17.070 --> 00:14:19.980
There's this framework
that this foundation,

00:14:19.980 --> 00:14:21.960
the Robin Hood Foundation,
which focuses on New York,

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:23.427
but it's a framework
that they came up with

00:14:23.427 --> 00:14:26.420
for calculating something
called social benefit

00:14:26.420 --> 00:14:27.520
to cost ratio.

00:14:27.520 --> 00:14:30.330
In the for profit world,
you look at something called

00:14:30.330 --> 00:14:32.370
return on investment, where you say, hey,

00:14:32.370 --> 00:14:35.680
if I invest $100 and I get $30 more,

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:39.140
and I get my $100 back, that's
a 30% return on investment.

00:14:39.140 --> 00:14:41.150
And so in my hedge fund
world, we're all back

00:14:41.150 --> 00:14:42.980
in the hedge fund days, I
would always think about,

00:14:42.980 --> 00:14:45.353
okay where we are we gonna
get the best financial return?

00:14:45.353 --> 00:14:47.110
Well in the not for profit world,

00:14:47.110 --> 00:14:48.700
where are you gonna get
the best impact return?

00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:50.590
How much impact for your dollar?

00:14:50.590 --> 00:14:53.740
And it's generally good
if you're able to get

00:14:53.740 --> 00:14:56.660
even a two to one impact to dollar ratio.

00:14:56.660 --> 00:14:58.110
So there's a social impact of two.

00:14:58.110 --> 00:14:59.840
And you say how do you
measure social impact?

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:02.090
Well you could say, if
someone's able to improve

00:15:02.090 --> 00:15:04.480
their academic standing by x amount,

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:06.560
how would that affect
their lifetime earnings?

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:09.719
How much will that decrease
costs in other parts of society?

00:15:09.719 --> 00:15:13.580
Education is inversely
correlated with how much

00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:15.650
you have to imprison people and crime,

00:15:15.650 --> 00:15:16.990
and all of these other things.

00:15:16.990 --> 00:15:18.930
And then there's tax revenues that you get

00:15:18.930 --> 00:15:20.720
the more educated people get.

00:15:20.720 --> 00:15:22.710
And so using proxies like that,

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:25.970
a very good social return
is about a 10 to one.

00:15:25.970 --> 00:15:29.940
Social benefit to cost ratios,
10 to one is a great one.

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:32.509
If you look at Khan Academy
and there's actually a video.

00:15:32.509 --> 00:15:34.630
I'm happy to share it, but it's a video

00:15:34.630 --> 00:15:36.970
where I do that back of
the envelope calculation.

00:15:36.970 --> 00:15:38.640
You'll see Khan Academy,
you get some place

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:41.400
between, actually,
pre-COVID, some place between

00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:44.040
a 250 to 400 to one ration.

00:15:44.040 --> 00:15:46.010
And post-COVID, now
that our usage is about

00:15:46.010 --> 00:15:48.810
two x, three x of what it typically is,

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:50.870
those numbers go up by two, three x.

00:15:50.870 --> 00:15:53.540
So you're talking about in
a world where good social

00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:55.860
benefit to cost ratio is two to one,

00:15:55.860 --> 00:15:58.260
three to one, we're talking
about a social benefit

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:01.110
to cost ratio which is like 500 to one.

00:16:01.110 --> 00:16:03.170
So for anyone donating even that dollar,

00:16:03.170 --> 00:16:05.310
you should feel very good about that.

00:16:05.310 --> 00:16:07.010
Even that one dollar.

00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:09.410
That's going to drive learning
for someone on the planet,

00:16:09.410 --> 00:16:12.440
probably someone who
doesn't have a dollar,

00:16:12.440 --> 00:16:15.616
to learn for a good amount of time.

00:16:15.616 --> 00:16:17.359
If I remember the numbers correctly,

00:16:17.359 --> 00:16:19.880
our cost of delivering learning

00:16:19.880 --> 00:16:22.170
is around 15 cents per hour.

00:16:22.170 --> 00:16:25.870
So even that dollar is able
to impact a lot of people.

00:16:25.870 --> 00:16:28.430
That $100 that you gave,
Sjwetel, there's gonna be a lot

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:30.670
of people that are gonna be
empowered because of that.

00:16:30.670 --> 00:16:32.730
So thank you so much for it.

00:16:32.730 --> 00:16:34.760
So I have more testimonials here.

00:16:34.760 --> 00:16:37.410
So this is from Heather, who's a parent.

00:16:37.410 --> 00:16:40.120
Heather writes, I've been
using Khan Academy for six

00:16:40.120 --> 00:16:45.120
of my children since the
onset of COVID-19 pandemic.

00:16:45.370 --> 00:16:47.640
I would have used it sooner
had I known it was available

00:16:47.640 --> 00:16:49.060
free of charge.

00:16:49.060 --> 00:16:50.830
Please donate if you can.

00:16:50.830 --> 00:16:53.670
It's truly beneficial to
families, especially now.

00:16:53.670 --> 00:16:56.330
Well thank you Heather
and we agree with you.

00:16:56.330 --> 00:16:59.530
One of the things, donate,
that would be huge.

00:16:59.530 --> 00:17:01.830
And hopefully you view yourself
as part of this mission

00:17:01.830 --> 00:17:03.160
and part of this community.

00:17:03.160 --> 00:17:04.910
For anyone out there make sure that,

00:17:04.910 --> 00:17:06.250
there's other parents like Heather

00:17:06.250 --> 00:17:07.930
who could benefit from
this, and their children

00:17:07.930 --> 00:17:10.280
could benefit, but there's
still an awful lot of people

00:17:10.280 --> 00:17:11.950
who don't know about Khan Academy.

00:17:11.950 --> 00:17:13.379
Or maybe they heard about Khan Academy

00:17:13.379 --> 00:17:15.890
and they think we're a
for profit organization.

00:17:15.890 --> 00:17:17.640
They think, oh that's just
another one of those ed tech

00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:19.750
companies that's trying to
get me on some five dollar

00:17:19.750 --> 00:17:21.430
a month plan or something like that.

00:17:21.430 --> 00:17:22.970
That is not who were are.

00:17:22.970 --> 00:17:24.825
We are there to give you free access

00:17:24.825 --> 00:17:27.010
to what we genuinely believe

00:17:27.010 --> 00:17:29.290
are the best materials out there.

00:17:29.290 --> 00:17:32.300
One of the logical fallacies
or emotional fallacies

00:17:32.300 --> 00:17:35.110
we sometimes see is people
think they get something better

00:17:35.110 --> 00:17:36.490
when they pay for it.

00:17:36.490 --> 00:17:38.560
And sometimes some of
these for profit companies

00:17:38.560 --> 00:17:39.870
are able to take advantage of that.

00:17:39.870 --> 00:17:41.210
They're like, oh Khan Academy's free,

00:17:41.210 --> 00:17:43.090
but we charge you 10 dollars a month,

00:17:43.090 --> 00:17:44.740
so you must think it's better.

00:17:44.740 --> 00:17:46.490
But do your research.

00:17:46.490 --> 00:17:49.130
The number of efficacy
studies done on Khan Academy,

00:17:49.130 --> 00:17:52.090
the level of transparency, our
ability to partner with folks

00:17:52.090 --> 00:17:54.273
like the college board around the SAT.

00:17:55.190 --> 00:17:57.950
If you objectively ask
knowledgeable people

00:17:57.950 --> 00:18:00.610
in the field, compare the
Khan Academy resources

00:18:00.610 --> 00:18:02.470
side by side with any of the things,

00:18:02.470 --> 00:18:05.510
even some of these things cost
hundreds of dollars a year,

00:18:05.510 --> 00:18:07.340
I think you'll find that
the Khan Academy resources

00:18:07.340 --> 00:18:11.020
are deeper, more aligned
with learning science,

00:18:11.020 --> 00:18:13.590
have much more efficacy research on it.

00:18:13.590 --> 00:18:15.680
Look at the Khan Academy Kids material.

00:18:15.680 --> 00:18:17.620
It's free, it's not commercial.

00:18:17.620 --> 00:18:20.240
I challenge anyone to find
anything that costs money

00:18:20.240 --> 00:18:22.967
that comes close to what the
team at Khan Academy Kids,

00:18:22.967 --> 00:18:24.539
and I brag about them a little bit more,

00:18:24.539 --> 00:18:28.680
because I can't claim that I did that,

00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:30.930
other than they kind of
joined forces with us.

00:18:30.930 --> 00:18:33.460
And I obviously helped fundraise for them

00:18:33.460 --> 00:18:36.080
and make sure that we're
aligned from a pedagogical

00:18:36.080 --> 00:18:37.050
and strategy point of view,

00:18:37.050 --> 00:18:38.400
but they've been doing incredible stuff.

00:18:38.400 --> 00:18:40.950
And obviously on the Khan
Academy, the core Khan Academy

00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:43.010
where I am more involved
and many, many other people

00:18:43.010 --> 00:18:45.900
are involved, I can't tell
you how much work is put in

00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:48.360
to make sure it's aligned
with learning science,

00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:50.570
make sure it's aligned to standards,

00:18:50.570 --> 00:18:52.310
and then do efficacy research on it

00:18:52.310 --> 00:18:55.860
and make sure we're impacting
as many students as possible.

00:18:55.860 --> 00:18:59.390
So more questions from AMA
questions, ask me anything

00:18:59.390 --> 00:19:00.440
questions from YouTube.

00:19:00.440 --> 00:19:03.549
Matius Ishimoto says,
and I'm reading these,

00:19:03.549 --> 00:19:05.420
so I don't know what this is about to say.

00:19:05.420 --> 00:19:07.630
Hi Sal, I wanna thank you and the board

00:19:07.630 --> 00:19:09.920
for bringing Khan Academy to Brazil.

00:19:09.920 --> 00:19:11.730
Can you explain why you
guys selected our country

00:19:11.730 --> 00:19:13.660
and your expectations for the future?

00:19:13.660 --> 00:19:15.621
So, great question, Matius.

00:19:15.621 --> 00:19:17.990
It's interesting, in the
early days of Khan Academy,

00:19:17.990 --> 00:19:19.355
obviously we've always had
this mission statement:

00:19:19.355 --> 00:19:22.510
free world class education
for anyone anywhere.

00:19:22.510 --> 00:19:24.900
And part of that vision is
we wanna make this available

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:26.534
to everyone on the planet.

00:19:26.534 --> 00:19:28.345
Localized, not just the videos,

00:19:28.345 --> 00:19:31.562
but the software platform,
the teacher tools.

00:19:31.562 --> 00:19:34.410
When we got our first funding in 2010,

00:19:34.410 --> 00:19:37.620
it was from the Gates
Foundation and Google.org.

00:19:37.620 --> 00:19:39.640
And the Google aspect of the fund,

00:19:39.640 --> 00:19:41.630
they said they wanted
us to start translating

00:19:41.630 --> 00:19:42.760
this into the languages of the world.

00:19:42.760 --> 00:19:44.790
So that's when we started back in 2010.

00:19:44.790 --> 00:19:47.410
And then I believe it was 2012,

00:19:47.410 --> 00:19:49.930
we had this gentleman
visit our offices out here

00:19:49.930 --> 00:19:51.210
in Mountain View from Brazil

00:19:51.210 --> 00:19:54.310
by the name of Jorge Paulo Lemann.

00:19:54.310 --> 00:19:57.640
And our offices, even now,
our offices aren't fancy

00:19:57.640 --> 00:19:59.886
by any stretch of the
imagination, but back then,

00:19:59.886 --> 00:20:03.300
my office was above a Chinese tea shop

00:20:03.300 --> 00:20:05.590
in downtown Mountain
View and it was kind of,

00:20:05.590 --> 00:20:07.350
you know, one of our first
funders walked in and said,

00:20:07.350 --> 00:20:10.184
Sal, this is not a healthy
working environment.

00:20:10.184 --> 00:20:13.320
She sent some painters and
handymen to just make it

00:20:13.320 --> 00:20:16.513
a vaguely decent environment.

00:20:17.364 --> 00:20:19.110
But Jorge Paulo visited that office.

00:20:19.110 --> 00:20:21.200
And if you're from Brazil,
you might have heard

00:20:21.200 --> 00:20:22.033
of Jorge Paulo.

00:20:22.033 --> 00:20:25.140
I'm ashamed to say that I
didn't know about Jorge Paulo.

00:20:25.140 --> 00:20:26.930
But he's actually one of
the biggest philanthropists

00:20:26.930 --> 00:20:29.840
in Brazil, and he and
his team came and said,

00:20:29.840 --> 00:20:32.820
we need to bring Khan Academy
to Brazil as soon as possible.

00:20:32.820 --> 00:20:35.560
And we were a five person
organization at the time.

00:20:35.560 --> 00:20:37.610
We were like, oh, well, we don't know how

00:20:37.610 --> 00:20:38.443
we're gonna be able to do that.

00:20:38.443 --> 00:20:40.620
And they're like, we can
support Khan Academy.

00:20:40.620 --> 00:20:43.110
We can help support the efforts in Brazil.

00:20:43.110 --> 00:20:45.000
And so the Lemann Foundation,

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:47.540
named after Jorge Paulo
Lemann, they helped support

00:20:47.540 --> 00:20:51.070
Khan Academy proper, and since
then have been great donors

00:20:51.070 --> 00:20:52.310
and supporters of us.

00:20:52.310 --> 00:20:55.030
But they've also, we've been
able to partner with them

00:20:55.030 --> 00:20:59.720
in Brazil to localize the work
into Brazilian Portuguese,

00:20:59.720 --> 00:21:02.290
align it with the Brazilian
national common core,

00:21:02.290 --> 00:21:05.490
do efficacy and research
studies in Brazilian schools.

00:21:05.490 --> 00:21:08.140
There's even a soap opera
episode in Brazil I heard,

00:21:08.140 --> 00:21:12.830
where the protagonist ran a
fundraiser for Khan Academy.

00:21:12.830 --> 00:21:14.920
So we have a lot of good
connections to Brazil.

00:21:14.920 --> 00:21:17.100
I visited there and had a wonderful time.

00:21:17.100 --> 00:21:19.187
I actually brought my whole family there.

00:21:19.187 --> 00:21:21.910
And worked with the Lemann folks.

00:21:21.910 --> 00:21:23.990
So that's what really
pulled us in to Brazil.

00:21:23.990 --> 00:21:28.080
So we have 45 translation
projects around the world.

00:21:28.080 --> 00:21:30.460
Most of it's volunteers
who are able to do it

00:21:30.460 --> 00:21:31.450
in their own languages.

00:21:31.450 --> 00:21:34.040
And they're unpaid, and your donations

00:21:34.040 --> 00:21:35.410
are allowing us to build a platform

00:21:35.410 --> 00:21:36.680
so that they can do it.

00:21:36.680 --> 00:21:39.170
But there's several
geographies that we're able

00:21:39.170 --> 00:21:40.003
to go deeper.

00:21:40.003 --> 00:21:42.000
Brazil is one of them, India's another,

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:43.560
and then Spanish speaking Latin America,

00:21:43.560 --> 00:21:45.200
especially places like Peru and Mexico.

00:21:45.200 --> 00:21:48.820
We have partners, Carlos
Slim Foundation in Mexico.

00:21:48.820 --> 00:21:52.580
And Carlos Rodriguez-Pastor
and his foundation in Peru.

00:21:52.580 --> 00:21:54.290
And Tata Trusts in India.

00:21:54.290 --> 00:21:56.843
These are all foundations
that have allowed us

00:21:56.843 --> 00:21:58.280
to go deeper.

00:21:58.280 --> 00:21:59.737
But I do wanna make it clear.

00:21:59.737 --> 00:22:02.977
All of these groups
have each been donating

00:22:02.977 --> 00:22:05.260
a lot of resources to make this happen,

00:22:05.260 --> 00:22:07.130
but for all of us to reach, literally

00:22:07.130 --> 00:22:08.590
the hundreds of millions of billions,

00:22:08.590 --> 00:22:10.455
we need more resources.

00:22:10.455 --> 00:22:13.760
And so it's a real
partnership with both large

00:22:13.760 --> 00:22:17.304
philanthropian foundations
and small philanthropists

00:22:17.304 --> 00:22:21.130
and people who can give,
even that $20, that $10.

00:22:21.130 --> 00:22:22.430
Or even those three dollars.

00:22:22.430 --> 00:22:24.373
It all makes a huge difference.

00:22:25.890 --> 00:22:28.850
A question from YouTube, Emmanuel Sowitz

00:22:28.850 --> 00:22:31.219
asks, how important do
you think coding will be

00:22:31.219 --> 00:22:34.083
for children's education in the future?

00:22:35.580 --> 00:22:37.350
I'm a big coding fan.

00:22:37.350 --> 00:22:41.350
My background in undergrad, I
was a computer science major.

00:22:41.350 --> 00:22:42.950
I always got a lot of joy out of coding.

00:22:42.950 --> 00:22:45.530
Coding empowered me to build

00:22:45.530 --> 00:22:47.290
the first versions of Khan Academy.

00:22:47.290 --> 00:22:49.160
Khan Academy wouldn't
exist if I didn't have

00:22:49.160 --> 00:22:52.270
some of those early desires
to prototype with coding.

00:22:52.270 --> 00:22:55.990
And so it can be a very,
very valuable skill.

00:22:55.990 --> 00:22:57.740
That's why we've invested on Khan Academy

00:22:57.740 --> 00:23:00.380
on our coding platform where
kids, and I encourage anyone,

00:23:00.380 --> 00:23:02.400
anyone who thinks that
coding is not for them,

00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:04.810
go to Khan Academy and
try our coding platform.

00:23:04.810 --> 00:23:05.950
You're gonna see that it focuses

00:23:05.950 --> 00:23:07.500
on the creative side of coding.

00:23:07.500 --> 00:23:09.250
You get to make pictures and animations,

00:23:09.250 --> 00:23:10.970
literally within minutes.

00:23:10.970 --> 00:23:12.840
But it takes you all the way down the path

00:23:12.840 --> 00:23:14.240
so that you can actually understand

00:23:14.240 --> 00:23:16.838
fairly sophisticated ideas
in computer programming

00:23:16.838 --> 00:23:18.570
and computer science.

00:23:18.570 --> 00:23:20.800
And so I think there's two tracks.

00:23:20.800 --> 00:23:24.770
I think there's a lot of the
need for software engineers,

00:23:24.770 --> 00:23:27.430
computer programmers,
it's only gonna increase.

00:23:27.430 --> 00:23:31.880
There's so many industries
that are going to leverage

00:23:31.880 --> 00:23:34.522
things like computing and data analytics,

00:23:34.522 --> 00:23:37.140
and data science, and
artificial intelligence

00:23:37.140 --> 00:23:38.630
more than they are today.

00:23:38.630 --> 00:23:40.820
So there's just gonna be more
and more, and more jobs there.

00:23:40.820 --> 00:23:42.290
So it's valuable for that.

00:23:42.290 --> 00:23:44.100
But even if someone isn't going to become

00:23:44.100 --> 00:23:47.240
a computer programmer, if
you're gonna become a doctor.

00:23:47.240 --> 00:23:48.570
If you're gonna become a lawyer.

00:23:48.570 --> 00:23:50.290
Whatever field, accountant.

00:23:50.290 --> 00:23:51.720
Whatever field someone goes in,

00:23:51.720 --> 00:23:53.300
almost every one of those industries

00:23:53.300 --> 00:23:56.430
are going to be affected
by computing going forward.

00:23:56.430 --> 00:23:59.100
So it's valuable to have at
least a baseline understanding

00:23:59.100 --> 00:24:02.970
of what computers can do,
how computing logic works.

00:24:02.970 --> 00:24:05.453
So that you can navigate
whatever field you go in to

00:24:05.453 --> 00:24:06.930
in the future.

00:24:06.930 --> 00:24:10.360
I'll also add that what's
fun about programming

00:24:10.360 --> 00:24:13.140
and computing is it helps
build a certain form

00:24:13.140 --> 00:24:14.870
of critical thinking and logic,

00:24:14.870 --> 00:24:17.290
which is, I think valuable in every field.

00:24:17.290 --> 00:24:22.100
So big picture, your
three Rs, so to speak,

00:24:22.100 --> 00:24:24.960
which were never Rs, three
Rs with two Rs and an A.

00:24:24.960 --> 00:24:26.750
Reading, writing, and arithmetic.

00:24:26.750 --> 00:24:28.500
Those are always going to be super core

00:24:28.500 --> 00:24:31.650
and there's going to be super
core for the 21st century.

00:24:31.650 --> 00:24:34.500
But I think computing builds
a lot of those same skills

00:24:34.500 --> 00:24:37.276
that the mathematics does in
terms of the critical thinking.

00:24:37.276 --> 00:24:41.610
And there's a creation side to it too,

00:24:41.610 --> 00:24:43.464
this engineering side
which is super valuable.

00:24:43.464 --> 00:24:46.567
If kids can get exposed to
it, I would love to see it

00:24:46.567 --> 00:24:48.510
and that's why we have
that on Khan Academy.

00:24:48.510 --> 00:24:51.400
We also have APCS principles
which is an advanced

00:24:51.400 --> 00:24:54.130
placement course which
is designed to be broad

00:24:54.130 --> 00:24:55.950
so that a lot of people
might want to take it.

00:24:55.950 --> 00:24:57.473
So you can go on to
Khan Academy right now.

00:24:57.473 --> 00:25:00.780
You could either learn to
program on our coding platform.

00:25:00.780 --> 00:25:03.132
Or you go to APCS principles
and I think almost anyone

00:25:03.132 --> 00:25:05.670
watching this could engage on it.

00:25:05.670 --> 00:25:07.610
Not only does it do the basics of coding,

00:25:07.610 --> 00:25:10.180
it's how do computers work,
how does the internet work.

00:25:10.180 --> 00:25:11.290
What is internet security?

00:25:11.290 --> 00:25:12.745
All of these things you
are always hearing about,

00:25:12.745 --> 00:25:16.350
we make available on Khan Academy.

00:25:16.350 --> 00:25:19.810
I definitely recommend
taking a look at some of that

00:25:19.810 --> 00:25:20.643
work as well.

00:25:21.794 --> 00:25:26.794
Let's see, there a testimonial
from AJ, who's a student.

00:25:27.240 --> 00:25:30.490
AJ wrote to us and says, no joke.

00:25:30.490 --> 00:25:32.440
The only reason I graduated college

00:25:32.440 --> 00:25:33.980
was because of Khan Academy.

00:25:33.980 --> 00:25:36.400
I pledged to myself that if I graduated,

00:25:36.400 --> 00:25:39.470
I would give every year after I graduated.

00:25:39.470 --> 00:25:41.920
I have kept that promise and
I am giving a little bit more

00:25:41.920 --> 00:25:44.750
during the COVID pandemic
because I want to make sure

00:25:44.750 --> 00:25:47.460
that everyone has the
opportunity to learn.

00:25:47.460 --> 00:25:49.730
Well AJ, thank you so much.

00:25:49.730 --> 00:25:51.190
That means a ton to us.

00:25:51.190 --> 00:25:54.900
I know, especially for
a young professional,

00:25:54.900 --> 00:25:56.060
you're out of college.

00:25:56.060 --> 00:25:57.300
You probably don't have a lot of money,

00:25:57.300 --> 00:25:58.980
but whatever you can donate
makes a big different.

00:25:58.980 --> 00:26:01.440
You know it makes a
different on two levels.

00:26:01.440 --> 00:26:03.670
The money obviously allows us to do work,

00:26:03.670 --> 00:26:05.780
allows us to hire people,
pay our server costs.

00:26:05.780 --> 00:26:08.880
Our server costs alone are
several million dollars a year.

00:26:08.880 --> 00:26:10.940
But also just that show of confidence.

00:26:10.940 --> 00:26:14.570
I can't tell you, that positive
energy that you send our way

00:26:14.570 --> 00:26:17.190
by supporting this makes a
big different to us as well.

00:26:17.190 --> 00:26:19.950
It energizes me and I know
it energizes the team.

00:26:19.950 --> 00:26:21.650
And it sets a very powerful example

00:26:21.650 --> 00:26:24.067
for hopefully other people to help donate

00:26:24.067 --> 00:26:27.510
to this cause that we think can empower

00:26:27.510 --> 00:26:29.853
billions of folks over generations.

00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:35.030
From YouTube, Life Fan asks,
Sal, if you have one million

00:26:35.030 --> 00:26:36.880
dollars, what will you do with it?

00:26:36.880 --> 00:26:39.330
And I'm assuming the
question is if Khan Academy

00:26:39.330 --> 00:26:40.530
had a million dollars.

00:26:40.530 --> 00:26:42.250
If I had a million dollars
I might think about

00:26:42.250 --> 00:26:43.650
donating it to Khan Academy.

00:26:44.665 --> 00:26:46.710
I'll answer your question.

00:26:46.710 --> 00:26:47.860
I'll go on a little tangent.

00:26:47.860 --> 00:26:49.860
A lot of people said,
Sal, how come Khan Academy

00:26:49.860 --> 00:26:51.500
isn't a for profit company?

00:26:51.500 --> 00:26:53.352
And I went through that thought process

00:26:53.352 --> 00:26:54.925
in the early days.

00:26:54.925 --> 00:26:57.320
Before I actually even talked
to any philanthropists,

00:26:57.320 --> 00:26:58.800
I live in the middle of Silicon Valley.

00:26:58.800 --> 00:27:00.720
Some of my friends are
venture capitalists.

00:27:00.720 --> 00:27:03.290
They offered, hey, I'll write
a $100,000 check right now.

00:27:03.290 --> 00:27:06.120
You give me X percent and
then you can quit your day job

00:27:06.120 --> 00:27:07.230
and we can turn Khan Academy

00:27:07.230 --> 00:27:08.950
into the next billion dollar company.

00:27:08.950 --> 00:27:11.540
And so you can imagine,
that was very tempting.

00:27:11.540 --> 00:27:14.230
But when I went through
the thought process,

00:27:14.230 --> 00:27:17.000
I was getting so much psychic
reward from folks like AJ

00:27:17.000 --> 00:27:18.690
saying how it helped them, and I said,

00:27:18.690 --> 00:27:21.220
well what if we had to
put a pay wall behind it?

00:27:21.220 --> 00:27:22.760
Or what if when someone
wants to learn algebra

00:27:22.760 --> 00:27:23.910
they had to watch an ad?

00:27:23.910 --> 00:27:26.360
And there's something that just
doesn't feel good about it.

00:27:26.360 --> 00:27:28.600
And I imagined what a
home run would look like

00:27:28.600 --> 00:27:29.610
in the for profit world.

00:27:29.610 --> 00:27:32.050
If it's a home run, it would
be a Google or a Facebook,

00:27:32.050 --> 00:27:32.883
or an Apple.

00:27:32.883 --> 00:27:33.830
And that's great.

00:27:33.830 --> 00:27:35.097
I think all three of those organizations

00:27:35.097 --> 00:27:37.330
do amazing work.

00:27:37.330 --> 00:27:40.730
They all innovate in the
world in amazing ways.

00:27:40.730 --> 00:27:42.090
But then I thought,
what would a home run be

00:27:42.090 --> 00:27:43.710
in the not for profit world?

00:27:43.710 --> 00:27:45.230
Well what if Khan Academy
could be that next

00:27:45.230 --> 00:27:47.150
great institution like the Smithsonian

00:27:47.150 --> 00:27:51.070
or the library system, or
the great universities?

00:27:51.070 --> 00:27:53.064
And it could be accessible by literally

00:27:53.064 --> 00:27:55.783
billions of people.

00:27:56.937 --> 00:27:58.980
When I started this I didn't have kids.

00:27:58.980 --> 00:27:59.840
Now I have three kids.

00:27:59.840 --> 00:28:01.608
But I thought about my
grandkids and their kids.

00:28:01.608 --> 00:28:05.960
What would they be more
proud of, of Khan Academy?

00:28:05.960 --> 00:28:09.260
And what could keep Khan
Academy sticking to its vision

00:28:09.260 --> 00:28:10.180
and it mission?

00:28:10.180 --> 00:28:12.330
And in my old career as
a hedge fund analyst,

00:28:12.330 --> 00:28:15.070
I saw how much the actions of a company

00:28:15.070 --> 00:28:17.360
are dictated by who owns the company.

00:28:17.360 --> 00:28:19.110
That you could have a
founder who wants to do

00:28:19.110 --> 00:28:21.250
very altruistic things, but
as soon as the ownership

00:28:21.250 --> 00:28:23.980
changes, it might go in a
completely different way,

00:28:23.980 --> 00:28:27.640
especially if it's owned by
firms that are just out there

00:28:27.640 --> 00:28:30.130
to maximize the buck.

00:28:30.130 --> 00:28:32.072
So I said okay, if I
want Khan Academy to last

00:28:32.072 --> 00:28:37.010
multiple generations, and
stay true to that mission,

00:28:37.010 --> 00:28:39.260
and that mission is its true bottom line,

00:28:39.260 --> 00:28:41.170
it's gotta be a not for profit.

00:28:41.170 --> 00:28:42.460
And I went through the thought process.

00:28:42.460 --> 00:28:44.470
Even if I were to start the next Google

00:28:44.470 --> 00:28:46.507
and become a billionaire,
I would just wanna donate

00:28:46.507 --> 00:28:49.290
that money to the effort
of education anyway.

00:28:49.290 --> 00:28:52.129
So lemme just cut out the middle man.

00:28:52.129 --> 00:28:56.040
And the benefit of a not for profit

00:28:56.040 --> 00:29:00.420
is any excess, we don't
have any right now,

00:29:00.420 --> 00:29:02.750
but if we were able to
not run at a deficit,

00:29:02.750 --> 00:29:03.850
it's not taxed.

00:29:03.850 --> 00:29:05.920
It would go towards a reserve.

00:29:05.920 --> 00:29:08.160
And so I said, well why,
just cut to the chase.

00:29:08.160 --> 00:29:10.060
Lemme just, if I can make a decent living

00:29:10.060 --> 00:29:11.180
as a professor, and I have to say,

00:29:11.180 --> 00:29:13.730
Khan Academy's board pays
me more than generously.

00:29:13.730 --> 00:29:16.010
I feel like I'm the luckiest
person on the planet

00:29:16.010 --> 00:29:19.150
that I get to be paid to do what I love.

00:29:19.150 --> 00:29:21.820
Anything above what I need for that,

00:29:21.820 --> 00:29:23.560
what those billions that could have been?

00:29:23.560 --> 00:29:25.970
I'm much happier that we're able to create

00:29:25.970 --> 00:29:29.190
an organization that has
impact for the broader world.

00:29:29.190 --> 00:29:31.410
And I know many of the
employees on Khan Academy,

00:29:31.410 --> 00:29:32.950
many of them are supporting this.

00:29:32.950 --> 00:29:33.930
They could go.

00:29:33.930 --> 00:29:35.730
Google is in the same town as we are.

00:29:35.730 --> 00:29:36.940
Facebook is down the street.

00:29:36.940 --> 00:29:38.620
Apple is a couple miles away.

00:29:38.620 --> 00:29:39.840
Pretty much all of them,

00:29:39.840 --> 00:29:41.535
if they were just optimizing for money,

00:29:41.535 --> 00:29:44.312
could go to those places and get stock,

00:29:44.312 --> 00:29:47.730
and probably double, their total comp,

00:29:47.730 --> 00:29:49.470
or whatever they're looking at.

00:29:49.470 --> 00:29:51.660
But they've all made the
decision that they want

00:29:51.660 --> 00:29:52.620
to work on this mission.

00:29:52.620 --> 00:29:54.506
So in a lot of ways,
they're also major donors

00:29:54.506 --> 00:29:56.573
to this organization.

00:29:57.910 --> 00:29:59.090
That was me going on a tangent,

00:29:59.090 --> 00:30:01.490
but that's where I obviously
feel quite passionately

00:30:01.490 --> 00:30:03.550
about the world needs something like this.

00:30:03.550 --> 00:30:05.483
And I can't imagine something more,

00:30:05.483 --> 00:30:09.086
I can't imagine being luckier
than being able to work

00:30:09.086 --> 00:30:11.210
on a mission like this.

00:30:11.210 --> 00:30:12.889
And now if the question
is what would Khan Academy

00:30:12.889 --> 00:30:17.810
do with that incremental
million dollars, it is,

00:30:17.810 --> 00:30:20.400
we wanna make sure that our
servers stay performant,

00:30:20.400 --> 00:30:24.130
that when you go, it doesn't
take seconds to load up.

00:30:24.130 --> 00:30:26.410
That it's a fast, great experience.

00:30:26.410 --> 00:30:28.250
We're always looking at how do we improve

00:30:28.250 --> 00:30:29.760
the user experience.

00:30:29.760 --> 00:30:31.120
For this coming back to school,

00:30:31.120 --> 00:30:33.670
we're looking at ways to maybe
do some quick diagnostics

00:30:33.670 --> 00:30:35.871
and ways for students to build
their foundations quickly.

00:30:35.871 --> 00:30:37.682
'Cause we know with a lot
of kids out of school,

00:30:37.682 --> 00:30:40.810
they're gonna have a huge
variance in preparedness.

00:30:40.810 --> 00:30:44.420
We would, every dollar, much
less every million dollars,

00:30:44.420 --> 00:30:46.975
allows us to think about
adding more subject areas,

00:30:46.975 --> 00:30:50.650
or improving the subject
areas that we already have.

00:30:50.650 --> 00:30:52.610
So those are just a sample of the things

00:30:52.610 --> 00:30:56.223
that we would do with every
incremental million dollars.

00:30:57.710 --> 00:31:00.670
So more donors I just wanna thank.

00:31:00.670 --> 00:31:04.320
So there's three people
donated anonymously, $140,

00:31:04.320 --> 00:31:06.647
so thank you three anonymous people.

00:31:06.647 --> 00:31:07.767
You know who you are.

00:31:07.767 --> 00:31:09.798
And thank you for that.

00:31:09.798 --> 00:31:12.450
Petko Carmochan donated on Facebook.

00:31:12.450 --> 00:31:13.700
Thank you so much, Petko.

00:31:15.280 --> 00:31:17.880
Sati Meta on Facebook wrote,

00:31:17.880 --> 00:31:19.130
thank you for making this available.

00:31:19.130 --> 00:31:21.240
It's amazing and my
eight year old loves it.

00:31:21.240 --> 00:31:23.340
Well, tell your eight
year old to keep going,

00:31:23.340 --> 00:31:25.640
and thank you Sati for that donation.

00:31:25.640 --> 00:31:28.050
From YouTube, Michelle Pally.

00:31:28.050 --> 00:31:31.110
Aloha, I'm assuming you're in Hawaii.

00:31:31.110 --> 00:31:32.421
Just donated on your website.

00:31:32.421 --> 00:31:33.830
Thank you so much.

00:31:33.830 --> 00:31:36.120
I am a teacher and use it religiously

00:31:36.120 --> 00:31:37.290
for students and myself.

00:31:37.290 --> 00:31:38.740
Well, Michelle, two things.

00:31:38.740 --> 00:31:39.670
Thank you for your donation

00:31:39.670 --> 00:31:41.790
and I would say this no matter what,

00:31:41.790 --> 00:31:43.440
because I love the work, I mean the work

00:31:43.440 --> 00:31:45.400
that educators do is the ultimate work.

00:31:45.400 --> 00:31:47.480
But especially given this is
Teacher Appreciation Week.

00:31:47.480 --> 00:31:50.030
Thank you Michelle for
the work you do and Aloha.

00:31:50.030 --> 00:31:52.870
I actually was in Florida
speaking at a teachers' conference

00:31:52.870 --> 00:31:54.695
last year and I learned a lot about,

00:31:54.695 --> 00:31:57.700
all of us non Hawaiians
know about the term Aloha,

00:31:57.700 --> 00:31:59.910
hello, goodbye, but
there's this whole notion

00:31:59.910 --> 00:32:02.839
of Aloha culture, which is
just how do you project love

00:32:02.839 --> 00:32:06.020
towards others and that really touched me.

00:32:06.020 --> 00:32:08.130
And so Aloha to you as well.

00:32:08.130 --> 00:32:10.049
Another anonymous donation, 50 dollars.

00:32:10.049 --> 00:32:13.723
You know who you are, thank you.

00:32:13.723 --> 00:32:17.520
Nashawa Shtelabi on Facebook,
thank you for your donation.

00:32:17.520 --> 00:32:19.950
And Nashawa wrote, thank
you for all your efforts.

00:32:19.950 --> 00:32:22.145
I use Khan Academy to
teach my three children.

00:32:22.145 --> 00:32:23.870
You are our pride and hope.

00:32:23.870 --> 00:32:25.990
Oh thank you so much, that means a lot.

00:32:25.990 --> 00:32:27.450
Keep up the great work.

00:32:27.450 --> 00:32:29.950
Sarah Holderman, thank
you for your donation.

00:32:29.950 --> 00:32:31.240
Every dollar matters

00:32:31.240 --> 00:32:33.720
and it means a ton to
all of us at Khan Academy

00:32:33.720 --> 00:32:35.710
that you're taking the trouble to donate.

00:32:35.710 --> 00:32:38.190
I know a lot of you all,
there's financial stress

00:32:38.190 --> 00:32:39.560
in the world.

00:32:39.560 --> 00:32:41.530
There's a lot of causes that need help,

00:32:41.530 --> 00:32:44.060
and they're all good, noble causes.

00:32:44.060 --> 00:32:46.600
But I thank all of you
all for appreciating

00:32:46.600 --> 00:32:48.705
that in this world right now education

00:32:48.705 --> 00:32:53.100
is very, very central and the access to it

00:32:53.100 --> 00:32:53.933
is very unequal.

00:32:53.933 --> 00:32:55.910
And I think hopefully
those of you all watching

00:32:55.910 --> 00:32:58.900
realize how powerful a
lever Khan Academy can be.

00:32:58.900 --> 00:33:00.934
And how much scale it has.

00:33:00.934 --> 00:33:03.420
As I said, we're the budget
of a large high school,

00:33:03.420 --> 00:33:07.690
but we're able to reach
a good chunk of humanity.

00:33:07.690 --> 00:33:10.978
And not just for this year,
but for generations to come.

00:33:10.978 --> 00:33:13.910
It's almost hard for the mind to grasp.

00:33:13.910 --> 00:33:15.050
But that's what gets us excited

00:33:15.050 --> 00:33:16.290
and that's what gets us energized

00:33:16.290 --> 00:33:17.990
to do the work that we need to do.

00:33:19.660 --> 00:33:22.190
We have another donation
just came on Facebook.

00:33:22.190 --> 00:33:24.270
Thank you so much, from anonymous.

00:33:24.270 --> 00:33:25.103
You know who you are.

00:33:25.103 --> 00:33:26.050
Maybe it's the same anonymous.

00:33:26.050 --> 00:33:30.274
Maybe it's different anonymouses, I guess?

00:33:30.274 --> 00:33:35.274
It looks like, unfortunately,
we're all out of time.

00:33:37.959 --> 00:33:41.000
I will, actually, I'll
take one more question,

00:33:41.000 --> 00:33:42.710
'cause I think this is very relevant.

00:33:42.710 --> 00:33:45.460
From Facebook, Gary Willmont asks,

00:33:45.460 --> 00:33:48.180
how would you describe the
company culture at Khan Academy,

00:33:48.180 --> 00:33:50.520
how has COVID-19 affected it?

00:33:50.520 --> 00:33:52.560
Well Gary, I'll tell you
something I told the team.

00:33:52.560 --> 00:33:54.250
We now have our company meetings now,

00:33:54.250 --> 00:33:55.920
as you can imagine, on Zoom.

00:33:55.920 --> 00:33:59.410
And one thing that the
leadership at Khan Academy

00:33:59.410 --> 00:34:00.600
has been telling all the team members

00:34:00.600 --> 00:34:04.410
is this is a time that you
always have to take care

00:34:04.410 --> 00:34:07.480
of yourself and your family
before you take care of others.

00:34:07.480 --> 00:34:09.330
We have team members, myself included,

00:34:09.330 --> 00:34:10.163
but I'm lucky.

00:34:10.163 --> 00:34:12.730
I have my wife and my
mother-in-law, we're all in the same

00:34:12.730 --> 00:34:14.870
house so we're able to
tag team with the kids.

00:34:14.870 --> 00:34:17.310
But we have team members who
might be a single parent.

00:34:17.310 --> 00:34:18.500
They have young children at home.

00:34:18.500 --> 00:34:19.990
It's incredibly hard.

00:34:19.990 --> 00:34:22.210
And in a time like that,
the last thing you need

00:34:22.210 --> 00:34:23.950
is for your employer to say, hey,

00:34:23.950 --> 00:34:26.070
I thought that thing that
you were gonna get done

00:34:26.070 --> 00:34:27.300
was gonna get done by this afternoon.

00:34:27.300 --> 00:34:28.140
Where is it?

00:34:28.140 --> 00:34:29.270
So we made it very clear.

00:34:29.270 --> 00:34:31.720
Some of our team members
are able to step up.

00:34:31.720 --> 00:34:32.940
They maybe have a little more time.

00:34:32.940 --> 00:34:35.250
And a lot of us are getting pulled

00:34:35.250 --> 00:34:36.652
in a lot of directions,
and so we're saying, hey,

00:34:36.652 --> 00:34:39.440
this is not a time that we're judging you.

00:34:39.440 --> 00:34:41.476
We know that the people who
are drawn to this mission

00:34:41.476 --> 00:34:44.310
are drawn to do the right thing.

00:34:44.310 --> 00:34:46.340
And we wanna figure out
how we can support you

00:34:46.340 --> 00:34:49.300
in any way, shape, or form that we can.

00:34:49.300 --> 00:34:51.570
And what I've told, I
told this to the team

00:34:51.570 --> 00:34:53.561
at Khan Academy yesterday.

00:34:53.561 --> 00:34:55.760
We have these aspirations.

00:34:55.760 --> 00:34:57.690
Very ambitious aspirations
for what we can do

00:34:57.690 --> 00:34:58.803
in the broader world.

00:34:58.803 --> 00:35:00.570
Let's educate a billion people.

00:35:00.570 --> 00:35:03.100
Let's create an institution
so that parents,

00:35:03.100 --> 00:35:04.439
teachers, students feel empowered

00:35:04.439 --> 00:35:08.320
for hundreds of years to come.

00:35:08.320 --> 00:35:11.210
But in order for that to
feel right for the users,

00:35:11.210 --> 00:35:14.370
we have to have the right energy
inside of our organization.

00:35:14.370 --> 00:35:16.470
If we're not energized,
if we're not passionate

00:35:16.470 --> 00:35:18.290
about what we do, that lack of passion

00:35:18.290 --> 00:35:20.540
is going to be carried on to the learners.

00:35:20.540 --> 00:35:23.250
We shouldn't do anything unless
we genuinely care about it.

00:35:23.250 --> 00:35:26.340
And so I emphasize with our team members

00:35:27.490 --> 00:35:29.140
work should not be work

00:35:29.140 --> 00:35:31.280
the way that a lot of
people think about it.

00:35:31.280 --> 00:35:32.750
A lot of people always think about, oh,

00:35:32.750 --> 00:35:33.870
my god, it's Monday morning.

00:35:33.870 --> 00:35:35.060
I guess I gotta go to work.

00:35:35.060 --> 00:35:36.780
I guess I gotta pay the bills.

00:35:36.780 --> 00:35:40.250
Work is where you spend
eight hours, nine hours,

00:35:40.250 --> 00:35:43.350
10 hours or more per day for
a good chunk of your life.

00:35:43.350 --> 00:35:45.280
It should be a place that fills your soul.

00:35:45.280 --> 00:35:46.990
It should be a place
that gives you meaning.

00:35:46.990 --> 00:35:49.460
It should be a place that
makes you feel connected

00:35:49.460 --> 00:35:52.170
with other people who are like-minded.

00:35:52.170 --> 00:35:54.270
And that's my aspirations for Khan Academy

00:35:54.270 --> 00:35:56.910
is that not only can we be, in some ways,

00:35:56.910 --> 00:35:59.600
pioneers on what could
happen for the world,

00:35:59.600 --> 00:36:01.933
but we could also show
examples of organizations

00:36:01.933 --> 00:36:05.490
that believe in people that are human,

00:36:05.490 --> 00:36:07.490
that are about filling your soul,

00:36:07.490 --> 00:36:12.100
that aren't political in
all of the kind of notions

00:36:12.100 --> 00:36:15.140
that you might be used
to or are stereotypical

00:36:15.140 --> 00:36:16.640
in some corporate settings.

00:36:16.640 --> 00:36:19.530
There can be a place where
you get the best out of people

00:36:19.530 --> 00:36:22.240
because every human being
has a natural desire

00:36:22.240 --> 00:36:23.750
to wanna help other people

00:36:23.750 --> 00:36:25.840
and a natural desire to
have impact on the world

00:36:25.840 --> 00:36:29.650
and tackle challenging
intellectual issues.

00:36:29.650 --> 00:36:33.350
And I think that's what we
hope to do at Khan Academy.

00:36:33.350 --> 00:36:36.150
So the culture I hope is, I believe to be,

00:36:36.150 --> 00:36:38.080
is very collaborative.

00:36:38.080 --> 00:36:39.730
I don't sense, really, politics,

00:36:39.730 --> 00:36:41.300
which I sensed at other organizations

00:36:41.300 --> 00:36:42.850
that I've worked in in my life.

00:36:42.850 --> 00:36:44.770
Because as I said, people
could go other places

00:36:44.770 --> 00:36:48.480
if they wanted to optimize
for income or titles.

00:36:48.480 --> 00:36:50.860
But people are coming here
to optimize for impact.

00:36:50.860 --> 00:36:52.420
And I think that just
creates a really, really

00:36:52.420 --> 00:36:54.823
positive culture inside
of an organization.

00:36:56.580 --> 00:37:00.024
I will now read, there's a last few folks

00:37:00.024 --> 00:37:02.619
who have donated.

00:37:02.619 --> 00:37:04.570
So another anonymous donation

00:37:04.570 --> 00:37:06.090
on YouTube, five dollars, thank you.

00:37:06.090 --> 00:37:08.430
As I said, every donation matters.

00:37:08.430 --> 00:37:12.970
Neela Mookergi, local,
donated on Facebook.

00:37:12.970 --> 00:37:14.823
Thank you so much, Neela.

00:37:14.823 --> 00:37:16.650
I really, really appreciate that.

00:37:16.650 --> 00:37:18.700
And the time went by fast.

00:37:18.700 --> 00:37:20.910
I guess time goes by fast when you're,

00:37:20.910 --> 00:37:22.660
I guess maybe I'm talking too much.

00:37:24.048 --> 00:37:25.798
I just wanna say again,

00:37:25.798 --> 00:37:28.720
thank you so much for all of
you who've donated in the past.

00:37:28.720 --> 00:37:31.170
Thank you so much for all
of you who donated now.

00:37:31.170 --> 00:37:33.420
This really makes a huge, huge difference

00:37:33.420 --> 00:37:35.120
for so many learners around the world.

00:37:35.120 --> 00:37:36.780
And it makes a huge
difference for our team

00:37:36.780 --> 00:37:40.050
and our capacity to do the
work that we want to do,

00:37:40.050 --> 00:37:42.230
and this is especially
true, now that we're in this

00:37:42.230 --> 00:37:45.488
COVID crisis, and the world
needs things like Khan Academy

00:37:45.488 --> 00:37:47.190
that much more.

00:37:47.190 --> 00:37:48.980
So thank you for being
on this journey with us.

00:37:48.980 --> 00:37:50.770
Tell everyone you know that we exist.

00:37:50.770 --> 00:37:52.720
We want to be used by more people

00:37:52.720 --> 00:37:55.682
so that we can grow our impact even more.

00:37:55.682 --> 00:38:00.310
And stay safe, stay healthy,
and I will see you tomorrow,

00:38:00.310 --> 00:38:03.070
where we're going to have,
actually, Angela Duckworth,

00:38:03.070 --> 00:38:05.500
famous education researcher on things like

00:38:05.500 --> 00:38:08.720
grit and resilience and
perseverance and a close friend

00:38:08.720 --> 00:38:11.770
of mine as well, on our
homeroom live stream.

00:38:11.770 --> 00:38:13.177
See you tomorrow.

00:39:43.410 --> 00:39:45.190
- Hello, (speaking foreign language).

00:39:45.190 --> 00:39:47.980
I like to use the schedule
provided by Khan Academy.

00:39:47.980 --> 00:39:51.072
My day includes doing a
math lesson on Khan Academy.

00:39:51.072 --> 00:39:53.960
Doing my homework on Google Classroom.

00:39:53.960 --> 00:39:56.110
And doing a guided meditation on YouTube.

00:39:56.110 --> 00:39:57.613
Thank you Khan Academy.

